WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:03.640
The delay in COVID-19 testing in the
United States meant that for weeks

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:07.320
doctors and public health officials were
flying blind about who was infected

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:10.660
and the location of major outbreaks. As a
result they had little hope

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:14.540
of containing the virus before it started
spreading out of control. Why did the US

00:00:14.549 --> 00:00:18.720
fall behind almost every other country
in this regard? It's a long-standing problem

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:23.220
that's led to needless suffering
and loss of life long before the Corona Virus pandemic.

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:27.060
But in this case its
impact is so high-profile and far-reaching

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:30.300
that the episode could finally bring lasting reform.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:35.860
Drastic measures being taken to try to contain the outbreak of a mysterious pneumonia-like virus.

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:39.140
The genetic sequence for COVID-19 was published on January 10th

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:43.400
by Chinese scientists and uploaded to
the National Institutes of Health's website

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:47.300
allowing laboratories around the
world to create their own diagnostic tests.

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:52.280
German scientists had one within a week, and other countries and private labs quickly followed suit.

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:57.700
The World Health Organization shipped 250,000 of the German version of the tests to labs around the world,

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:02.320
but the US Center for Disease Control decided it needed to rely on its own version of the test,

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:07.420
which would be certified by the Food and
Drug Administration, but it wasn't ready until early February.

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:12.356
February 6th and 7th, ninety tests were shipped to state public health labs around the country,

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:16.620
but the kits had a technical flaw and needed to be returned to the CDC for testing.

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:18.660
Meanwhile the virus continued spreading.

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:21.860
On February 4th, the FDA said it
would allow government-approved labs

00:01:21.869 --> 00:01:25.320
with high complexity testing
capabilities to create their own tests,

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:29.130
provided they copy the CDC's approach
and send all of their results to the

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:33.659
agency's headquarters in Atlanta for
verification. Former FDA Principal Deputy

00:01:33.659 --> 00:01:37.079
Commissioner Joshua Sharfstein told the New Yorker you certainly wouldn't want

00:01:37.079 --> 00:01:40.860
to say any lab can advertise a
coronavirus test because then it's going

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:44.130
to be chaos there's a lot of people who
will sell things that may or may not

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:48.149
work though well-intentioned the rule
became a stumbling block every virus a

00:01:48.149 --> 00:01:52.259
little different but this is one of the
more most challenging ones certainly

00:01:52.259 --> 00:01:56.130
I've seen in my lifetime take the case
of Alex Gruninger a doctor and

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:59.340
researcher at the University of
Washington who sent in his application

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:03.960
to create a corona virus test via email
according to a report in GQ he then

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:07.320
learned that he also needed to submit a
paper copy and then another version

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:11.280
burned to a compact disc or loaded onto
a drive and delivered to the FDA's

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:14.250
maryland headquarters after he complied
according to

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:18.030
reportin Republica the fda didn't
approve his test right away they asked

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:21.480
him to make sure his tests didn't cross
diagnosed with SARS and MERS other

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:25.560
coronaviruses which hadn't been seen in
the US and years his test was finally

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:29.100
certified on February 29th
at which point the fatal outbreak in his

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:35.880
home state of Washington was already
underway as the crisis worsened in the

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:39.990
testing shortage drew headlines the FDA
simplified the process but then on March

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:43.320
20th they warned that they may shut down
efforts to rapidly make available at

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:46.410
home testing kits on the grounds that
they were unvetted and could be

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:50.820
fraudulent the first documented cases of
joven 19 in the u.s. and South Korea

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:55.410
were discovered around the same time yet
as of March 17 one out of 4,300

00:02:55.410 --> 00:03:01.800
Americans had been tested in South Korea
it's 1 out of 17 had diagnostic tests

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:04.770
for Cova 19 been available sooner they
would have helped public health

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:08.640
officials isolate quarantine and provide
medical supplies to areas of the u.s.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:15.730
affected by the virus

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:20.520
how could this happen this tragic
testing delay has brought national

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:24.360
attention to the FDA's long-standing
overly cautious approach to regulating

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:28.440
American health care to minimize risk
the agency is sacrificed speed

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:32.550
preventing doctors and patients from
accessing the tools they need until it's

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:36.600
too late the agency's risk reduction
over speed of approval goes back to the

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:40.500
1960s when a sleeping pill called
thalidomide marketed to pregnant women

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:44.580
cause babies to die in utero or to be
born with serious birth defects the

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:48.360
crisis was mostly contained to Europe
because concerned FDA regulators have

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:52.950
kept the untested drug out of the u.s.
United faith and the best and the most

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:57.570
effective food and drug law of any
country in the world in 1962 President

00:03:57.570 --> 00:04:01.590
Kennedy signed a landmark bill
increasing the agency's oversight powers

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:05.790
it resulted in a culture of extreme
caution that led to long approval times

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:09.810
for experimental drugs and devices
keeping potentially life-saving tools

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:13.310
out of the hands of patients

00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:18.060
the beginning of the movement to relax
those roles and rethink the trade-off

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:21.660
between risk and speed in the FDA
approval process began during the AIDS

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:25.919
crisis of the 1980s gay rights activists
were outraged that the FDA wouldn't

00:04:25.919 --> 00:04:31.110
quickly approve more experimental drugs
like AZT which blocks HIV replication or

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:35.130
Athol it amide used to treat some of the
wasting symptoms of AIDS after lobbying

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:39.690
in protests including the 1988 shutdown
of the FDA building the administration

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:43.350
slowly began to loosen its rules leading
to the approval of highly active

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:49.110
antiretroviral therapy or Hart in 1996
because of Hart contracting HIV wasn't a

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:52.710
death sentence anymore
gay rights activism during the 1980s and

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:56.400
1990s created a framework for patient
advocacy to people with life-threatening

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:59.850
illnesses leading to the right to try
movement which has allowed terminally

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:03.360
ill patients to access experimental
drugs and devices that are still

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:07.350
undergoing FDA testing the movement
pushed the agency to streamline its own

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:12.660
compassionate use program to help the
same kinds of patients but the Cova 19

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:16.290
testing debacle underscores the limits
of those reforms once a full

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:19.530
investigation comes out about the
agency's failures perhaps it will bring

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:24.030
fundamental reform at last giving
Americans rapid access to potentially

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:26.840
life-saving tools

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:31.110
you

